🔷 Architecture Summary
✅ Components:
4 CCP Nodes

2 located in HQ Chicago

2 located in Dallas DR site

F5 LTM Load Balancer with a dedicated VIP

Exposed via DNS: aam.company.com

Health Check Mechanism:

F5 monitors each node by checking the content of an index.html file under the wwwroot directory on each CCP host.

Admins manually toggle availability by editing this file to return Online or Offline.

🔄 Traffic Flow Behavior
Client Application connects to aam.company.com.

F5 LTM resolves aam.company.com to the configured VIP.

F5 evaluates the health check of each backend CCP node by reading its index.html.

If index.html returns:

Online → Node is considered healthy and included in load balancing.

Offline → Node is marked unavailable, and traffic is not routed to it.

Requests are load balanced across the available CCP nodes (Chicago and Dallas) using configured F5 logic (e.g., round-robin, least connections, etc.).

🔧 Manual Node Maintenance
When a CyberArk Admin needs to take a CCP node out of the VIP (e.g., for upgrade or patching):

They access the node.

Navigate to: C:\inetpub\wwwroot\index.html

Change its contents to Offline.

F5 will detect this in its next health check interval and stop forwarding traffic to that node.

🏗️ Design Advantages
Feature	Benefit
Geographic node distribution (Chicago & Dallas)	High availability and DR support
DNS VIP aam.company.com	Simplified client integration
HTML-based node control	Simple, manual failover/maintenance process without F5 config changes
F5 health monitoring	Dynamic node selection and failover
